<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<bpmn:definitions xmlns:bpmn="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/MODEL"
                  xmlns:bpmndi="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/DI"
                  xmlns:dc="http://www.omg.org/spec/DD/20100524/DC"
                  xmlns:di="http://www.omg.org/spec/DD/20100524/DI"
                  xmlns:folio="https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/bpmn"
                  id="Definitions_Process_CRDM_DataModel" targetNamespace="https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/workflows"
                  exporter="folio-assistant" exporterVersion="1">
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CRDM data-modelling phase. Owner, 2026-09-20: "and to data
       modeling guidance in skills for CRDM when data modelling comes
       in (does subprocess exist yet?)" — it did, and CRDM was already
       built on it, so this is a fifth callActivity rather than new
       machinery.

       WHY BETWEEN REQUIREMENTS AND SIGN-OFF. Requirements are
       statements about things; the model says what the things ARE.
       Drafted earlier it is invented from nothing, since the entities
       come from the phase-2 BPA. Drafted later, phase 5 signs off
       requirements whose nouns were never pinned — which is how two
       stakeholders approve one sentence meaning different things.

       TWO LANES CONFIRM, AND THEY ARE ASKED DIFFERENT QUESTIONS. The
       BA answers "are these the things?", a naming question they own
       by owning the vocabulary. The stakeholders answer "is it really
       one per?", an operational question that the people running the
       process can answer and the BA often cannot. Collapsing them
       into one review is how a wrong cardinality ships — it is
       invisible until the data arrives.

       TWO SKILLS, NOT ONE, per the owner's "twoskills". Every
       activity here names `crdm-data-model`, the PHASE skill. The
       technique it invokes, `data-modelling`, carries no CRDM at all
       so a folio modelling data outside a requirements process can
       read it without inheriting a methodology it is not running.
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <bpmn:collaboration id="Collaboration_Process_CRDM_DataModel">
    <bpmn:participant id="Participant_Process_CRDM_DataModel" name="CRDM data model — entities, relations, cardinalities" processRef="Process_CRDM_DataModel"/>
  </bpmn:collaboration>
  <bpmn:process id="Process_CRDM_DataModel" name="CRDM data model" isExecutable="false">
    <bpmn:documentation>Draft the data model from the BPA and the requirements, have the BA confirm the entities and the stakeholders confirm the cardinalities, and loop until both. A requirement the model cannot express is a finding, not a modelling failure: it means the requirement is about something nobody has named.</bpmn:documentation>
    <bpmn:extensionElements>
      <!-- STRICT, declared rather than defaulted. The step an agent
           skips under time pressure is the cardinality confirmation —
           it is slow, it needs people, and the model looks finished
           without it. Skipping it is exactly how a model ships with a
           hole where the exceptions live. -->
      <folio:policy enforcement="strict"/>
    </bpmn:extensionElements>
    <bpmn:laneSet id="LaneSet_Process_CRDM_DataModel">
      <bpmn:lane id="Lane_BA" name="BA / Feature Requestor">
        <bpmn:documentation>Confirms naming only — are these the entities meant — and stops there: Lane_Stakeholders asks the separate, operational question about cardinality next, and the two are kept apart on purpose, because collapsing them into one review is how a wrong cardinality ships invisibly.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:extensionElements><folio:role ref="business-analyst"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>BA_ConfirmEntities</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
      </bpmn:lane>
      <bpmn:lane id="Lane_Agent" name="Agent">
        <bpmn:documentation>Does the actual modelling — entities, relations, and the declaration that makes the model machine-readable rather than prose — and absorbs the entire cost of a revise: the gate loops back to A_Entities whichever confirmation failed, BA's or the stakeholders', so this lane cannot assume a cardinality objection leaves its entities untouched.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:extensionElements><folio:role ref="authoring-agent"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Start_Process_CRDM_DataModel</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>A_Entities</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>A_Relations</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>A_Declare</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>GW_Model</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>End_Process_CRDM_DataModel</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
      </bpmn:lane>
      <bpmn:lane id="Lane_Stakeholders" name="Stakeholders">
        <bpmn:documentation>Answers the operational half the BA cannot — not what a thing is but how many of it there really is — because that is knowable only to the people who run the process day to day. It is also the step this process's STRICT policy exists for: skipped under time pressure, a model looks finished without it, which is exactly how a hole where the exceptions live would ship unnoticed.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:extensionElements><folio:role ref="stakeholder"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>S_ConfirmCardinality</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
      </bpmn:lane>
    </bpmn:laneSet>
    <bpmn:startEvent id="Start_Process_CRDM_DataModel" name="Requirements agreed"/>
    <bpmn:task id="A_Entities" name="Identify entities&#10;from the BPA and requirements">
      <bpmn:documentation>Every verb in the BPA whose object is a candidate entity, and every noun in a requirement. An entity with no identifier is an attribute of something else — that test is decisive and belongs to the technique skill.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements><folio:skill ref="crdm-data-model"/><folio:raci ref="business-analyst" involvement="accountable"/><folio:raci ref="stakeholder" involvement="consulted"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
    </bpmn:task>
    <bpmn:task id="A_Relations" name="Relations and&#10;cardinalities, both ways">
      <bpmn:documentation>Stated in both directions, because "one attestation per report" and "one report per attestation" are different claims and a model with only the first cannot say whether a re-signature is an update or a second row.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements><folio:skill ref="crdm-data-model"/><folio:raci ref="business-analyst" involvement="accountable"/><folio:raci ref="stakeholder" involvement="consulted"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
    </bpmn:task>
    <bpmn:task id="BA_ConfirmEntities" name="Confirm the entities&#10;are the ones meant">
      <bpmn:documentation>A naming question. The BA owns the domain vocabulary; the agent does not guess, which is CRDM's own rule for phase 1 and applies here with more force.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements><folio:skill ref="crdm-data-model"/><folio:raci ref="business-analyst" involvement="accountable"/><folio:raci ref="stakeholder" involvement="informed"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
    </bpmn:task>
    <bpmn:task id="S_ConfirmCardinality" name="Confirm the&#10;cardinalities">
      <bpmn:documentation>An operational question, asked of the people who run the process because they know where the exceptions are.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements><folio:skill ref="crdm-data-model"/><folio:raci ref="business-analyst" involvement="accountable"/><folio:raci ref="authoring-agent" involvement="informed"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
    </bpmn:task>
    <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="GW_Model" name="Entities recognised and&#10;every cardinality answered?">
      <bpmn:documentation>Answered by the BA after confirming cardinalities: are the entities recognised and every cardinality answered? `revise` returns to identifying entities; `yes` declares the model where a tool can read it.</bpmn:documentation>
    </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
    <bpmn:task id="A_Declare" name="Declare the model&#10;where a tool can read it">
      <bpmn:documentation>A model in prose is a proposal; a model in the declaration is a model. Ship a reader in the same change — everything here that was only documentation went inert within weeks.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements><folio:skill ref="crdm-data-model"/><folio:raci ref="business-analyst" involvement="accountable"/><folio:raci ref="publication-manager" involvement="informed"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
    </bpmn:task>
    <bpmn:endEvent id="End_Process_CRDM_DataModel" name="Model declared"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_1" sourceRef="Start_Process_CRDM_DataModel" targetRef="A_Entities"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_2" sourceRef="A_Entities" targetRef="A_Relations"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_3" sourceRef="A_Relations" targetRef="BA_ConfirmEntities"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_4" sourceRef="BA_ConfirmEntities" targetRef="S_ConfirmCardinality"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_5" sourceRef="S_ConfirmCardinality" targetRef="GW_Model"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_6" name="revise" sourceRef="GW_Model" targetRef="A_Entities"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_7" name="yes" sourceRef="GW_Model" targetRef="A_Declare"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_8" sourceRef="A_Declare" targetRef="End_Process_CRDM_DataModel"/>
  </bpmn:process>
  <bpmndi:BPMNDiagram id="Diagram_Process_CRDM_DataModel">
    <bpmndi:BPMNPlane id="Plane_Process_CRDM_DataModel" bpmnElement="Collaboration_Process_CRDM_DataModel">
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_Participant_Process_CRDM_DataModel" bpmnElement="Participant_Process_CRDM_DataModel" isHorizontal="true">
        <dc:Bounds x="160" y="80" width="1666" height="584" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_Lane_BA_Process_CRDM_DataModel" bpmnElement="Lane_BA" isHorizontal="true">
        <dc:Bounds x="190" y="80" width="1636" height="170" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_Lane_Agent_Process_CRDM_DataModel" bpmnElement="Lane_Agent" isHorizontal="true">
        <dc:Bounds x="190" y="250" width="1636" height="170" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_Lane_Stakeholders_Process_CRDM_DataModel" bpmnElement="Lane_Stakeholders" isHorizontal="true">
        <dc:Bounds x="190" y="420" width="1636" height="170" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_Start_Process_CRDM_DataModel" bpmnElement="Start_Process_CRDM_DataModel">
        <dc:Bounds x="250" y="317" width="36" height="36" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_A_Entities" bpmnElement="A_Entities">
        <dc:Bounds x="350" y="295" width="140" height="80" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_A_Relations" bpmnElement="A_Relations">
        <dc:Bounds x="550" y="295" width="140" height="80" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_BA_ConfirmEntities" bpmnElement="BA_ConfirmEntities">
        <dc:Bounds x="750" y="125" width="140" height="80" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_S_ConfirmCardinality" bpmnElement="S_ConfirmCardinality">
        <dc:Bounds x="950" y="465" width="140" height="80" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_GW_Model" bpmnElement="GW_Model" isMarkerVisible="true">
        <dc:Bounds x="1170" y="310" width="50" height="50" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_A_Declare" bpmnElement="A_Declare">
        <dc:Bounds x="1300" y="295" width="140" height="80" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_End_Process_CRDM_DataModel" bpmnElement="End_Process_CRDM_DataModel">
        <dc:Bounds x="1540" y="317" width="36" height="36" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="Edge_SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_1" bpmnElement="SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_1">
        <di:waypoint x="286" y="335" />
        <di:waypoint x="350" y="335" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="Edge_SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_2" bpmnElement="SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_2">
        <di:waypoint x="490" y="335" />
        <di:waypoint x="550" y="335" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="Edge_SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_3" bpmnElement="SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_3">
        <di:waypoint x="690" y="335" />
        <di:waypoint x="720" y="335" />
        <di:waypoint x="720" y="165" />
        <di:waypoint x="750" y="165" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="Edge_SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_4" bpmnElement="SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_4">
        <di:waypoint x="890" y="165" />
        <di:waypoint x="920" y="165" />
        <di:waypoint x="920" y="505" />
        <di:waypoint x="950" y="505" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="Edge_SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_5" bpmnElement="SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_5">
        <di:waypoint x="1090" y="505" />
        <di:waypoint x="1140" y="505" />
        <di:waypoint x="1140" y="335" />
        <di:waypoint x="1170" y="335" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="Edge_SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_6" bpmnElement="SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_6">
        <di:waypoint x="1195" y="360" />
        <di:waypoint x="1195" y="610" />
        <di:waypoint x="420" y="610" />
        <di:waypoint x="420" y="375" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="Edge_SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_7" bpmnElement="SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_7">
        <di:waypoint x="1220" y="335" />
        <di:waypoint x="1300" y="335" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="Edge_SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_8" bpmnElement="SF_Process_CRDM_DataModel_8">
        <di:waypoint x="1440" y="335" />
        <di:waypoint x="1540" y="335" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
    </bpmndi:BPMNPlane>
  </bpmndi:BPMNDiagram>
</bpmn:definitions>
